      "notes": "C6 pass on a reduced condition set, the path the pre-registration explicitly allows (a point is resolved by a third pass or the study's condition set is reduced, and either outcome is recorded). Kept 28 separable points (non-saturated markers plus isolated saturated markers); dropped the eight narrow_occluded_uncertain leftovers (three on panel b, five on panel d) after adjudication against pass 1x. Section III.C's nine measurements below 1 mGy are the paper's count: seven are in the kept set, two remain in the occluded 0.66-0.70 mGy pile whose cardinality cannot be recovered independently of method. Saturated Az=1.000 stacks have no rank information and are not kept as clusters. x (dose) is the pass-2 axis calibration pending Yamamoto WebPlotDigitizer pass 1; y is the pass-1x value where one exists, otherwise pass 2, never the average. Max deviation on the reduced unsaturated matches 0.81%. Full record: docs/h2_cand04_condition_set_reduction.md."
    }
  ],
  "screened": [
    {
      "study_id": "goo2004",
      "citation": "Goo JM, et al. Effect of monitor luminance and ambient light on observer performance in soft-copy reading of digital chest radiographs. Radiology 2004;232(3). doi:10.1148/radiol.2323030628 (PMID 15273338). Candidate CAND-14 of docs/h2_candidate_scan_v1.1.md; bibliographic detail confirmed at title and abstract level only.",
      "failed_criterion": "C2",
      "exclusion_reason": "C2: 'ambient_light' correspond to no term in the model, so the effect of varying them cannot be predicted; an axis that cannot be predicted is not counted as a validation axis (pre-registration v1.2 section A). The study varies monitor luminance and ambient light; 'luminance' is an admissible axis under v1.2 but leaves one effective axis, short of the two C2 requires. Decided on the abstract's stated design, before any performance data was read. A second concern is recorded in the candidate scan and deliberately kept out of this reason so that the exclusion is not retrofitted with stacked justifications: observers were free to adjust window width and level, which the model takes as a fixed input."
    },
    {
      "study_id": "macmahon1986",
      "citation": "MacMahon H, Vyborny CJ, Metz CE, Doi K, Sabeti V, Solomon SL. Digital radiography of subtle pulmonary abnormalities: an ROC study of the effect of pixel size on observer performance. Radiology 1986;158:21-26. Candidate CAND-07; judged on the full text.",
      "failed_criterion": "C2",
      "exclusion_reason": "C2: pixel size is the only condition axis varied (1.0, 0.5, 0.2 and 0.1 mm, against the undigitised original), which is one axis where C2 requires two. The two detection tasks (interstitial infiltrate, pneumothorax) are separate experiments with different observer panels and different viewing-time limits, and abnormality type is not a condition axis: it corresponds to no model input, and the model has no term for either a diffuse infiltrate pattern or a line shadow. Recorded separately and not part of the reason: A_z is reported only in ROC-curve figures and in Figure 6, with no table, so C6 would have required digitisation had C2 been met. This is a Kurt Rossmann Laboratories study, cited in section 3.2 of the protocol as the lineage this work positions itself against; excluding it leaves the non-CT generality slot open."
    },
    {
      "study_id": "herron2000",
      "citation": "Herron JM, Bender TM, Campbell WL, Sumkin JH, Rockette HE, Gur D. Effects of Luminance and Resolution on Observer Performance with Chest Radiographs. Radiology 2000;215(1):169-174. doi:10.1148/radiology.215.1.r00ap34169. Candidate CAND-08; judged on the full text.",
      "failed_criterion": "C3",
      "exclusion_reason": "C3: the study has no fully specified condition points. Its two axes are admissible under v1.2 (luminance at 85, 260 and 770 cd/m2; pixel size at 400, 200 and 100 micrometres) and it states that AUC was computed for all nine modes, but the published tables report only marginals: Table 1 averages over luminance and Table 2 averages over resolution, while Tables 3 to 5 resolve individual readers along luminance alone. A value averaged over an axis is not a point in condition space, and the model predicts detectability at a stated luminance and pixel size rather than at an average over either, so the count of usable condition points is zero against the four C3 requires. Recorded separately and not part of the reason: along the resolution marginal the reported AUC values for the nodule task differ by 0.01 or less, so even the marginals carry almost no ordering. This was the first pick for the generality slot, and v1.2 admitting luminance as an axis is what let it get as far as C3; excluding it empties the non-CT slot, which works against the paper's generality claim rather than for it."
    }
  ]
}
